<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Classroom News</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Will Write the Article About Our School Trip.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: text: Explain the main points of class readings to peers. communication: Assist in resolving misunderstandings among peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can assist peers in resolving misunderstandings by rephrasing and using visuals (e.g., writing 'First, write; then, draw,' with matching pictures). Can explain the main points of class readings by summarizing key ideas with visuals (e.g., writing 'school trip' and pointing to a bus and museum drawing). Can support classmates in understanding newsletter themes by writing and showing visuals (e.g., writing 'school events' and pointing to drawings of class activities).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of various sections from a school newsletter, such as a headline, an article about a school trip, and an announcement for an upcoming event. The teacher asks, "What is this part about?" and "What did we learn about the school trip yesterday?" Students orally recall key facts. The teacher then asks, "If a new student wasn't here for the trip, how can we *explain* it to them?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to explain and rephrase. The teacher points to a picture of the school bus from the trip and says, "Yesterday, we went on a field trip to the Emberá Community." Then, the teacher models mediation: "This means 'Yesterday, we took a bus to visit the Emberá people.' We learned about their culture." The teacher emphasizes the 'go' in 'I'll go' when planning. The teacher demonstrates writing 'school trip' and pointing to a bus and museum drawing to summarize key ideas.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If your friend doesn't understand 'article,' what can you do to help them?" (Expected: "I can explain it," "I can show a picture.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is explaining about new things or about things we already know?" (Expected: "Things we already know.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Classroom News".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a simple, short written draft of an article about the school trip, adapted for a 6th-grade newsletter (e.g., "Yesterday, we visited the Emberá Community. We saw beautiful art and learned about their history. It was a fun and meaningful event."). The teacher also displays a set of simple instructions for creating a "Digital Classroom Newsletter," outlining roles and steps (e.g., "1. Write the article. 2. Collect pictures. 3. Design the layout."). The instructions will be short and use future tense (e.g., "I will write the article," "We will take photos").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Collaborate in writing projects, such as creating a classroom newsletter, by clarifying roles, responsibilities, and deadlines, and using appropriate language for teamwork (e.g., 'I will be responsible for the layout of the page.' 'Can you write the article about our school trip?' 'Let's decide who will collect the pictures.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "What will we write about in the newsletter?" (Gist)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match newsletter roles (e.g., 'editor', 'photographer', 'writer') to their responsibilities from the presented instructions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False: "The article is about a future event." (False, it's past.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice explaining, rephrasing, and summarizing using present simple questions, future tense, past simple, and target vocabulary (article, newsletter, event, pictures, explain, describe, share, exciting, fun). Focus on identifying short and long vowel sounds (e.g., /ɑ/ in 'art', /oʊ/ in 'show').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Visual Summary Matching:</b> Students receive short paragraphs about recent class events (from previous lessons) and a set of simple drawings (e.g., a student writing, a group discussing). In pairs, they must match each paragraph to the visual that best summarizes its main idea. They write sentences like, "This paragraph explains 'classroom events,' and this drawing shows 'students working together'." The teacher reinforces: "We're matching the main idea, like explaining with pictures."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Rephrase and Show:</b> The teacher writes a few sentences on the board related to the newsletter project: "We need pictures for the classroom newsletter." "I will write the article." "Yesterday, we went to the museum." Students work in pairs. One student reads a sentence. The other student must rephrase it simply and use a quick drawing or gesture to illustrate the main point. For example, for "We need pictures for the classroom newsletter," a student might say, "We require photos for our news page" and draw a camera. They practice emphasizing the verb in future contractions, e.g., "I'll *write*."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>"Explain the Task" Peer Check:</b> Students receive cards with one specific instruction for the digital newsletter (e.g., "Write a headline for the school trip," "Organize the layout of the event page"). In pairs, Student A reads their instruction. Student B must *explain* what Student A will do, using simpler words if needed, and ask clarifying questions like "What else do we need?" or "When did this happen?" Student A then confirms or corrects.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Classroom News".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students) to finalize their "Digital Classroom Newsletter" project section. Each group has been assigned a specific section (e.g., "School Trip Article," "Upcoming Events," "Student Art Column").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Each group will present their section of the digital newsletter to another group (or the whole class). One student from the group acts as the primary explainer, summarizing the main points of their section. Another student acts as the "clarifier," ready to rephrase information or answer questions from the audience, ensuring everyone understands. They must use visuals (e.g., a simple poster of their section, quick drawings on a whiteboard, or showing their digital draft) to support their explanations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group briefly presents their newsletter section. The designated explainer states: "We will share our article about the school trip. Yesterday, we went to the Emberá Community." They use sentences like, "This paragraph describes our fun day," or "This photo shows the beautiful art."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The audience group listens and asks questions. For instance, "What will you do next for the layout?" or "Can you explain 'meaningful'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The designated clarifier from the presenting group must rephrase the explanation or assist in resolving misunderstandings. E.g., "Meaningful means it was important and we learned a lot." or "First, we will finish writing; then, we will add pictures." They use visuals like writing 'First, write; then, draw,' with matching pictures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups rotate roles.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist. The teacher will circulate and observe each group's presentation and clarification, noting down students' ability to explain, rephrase, and use visuals effectively.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their group presentation and clarification activity from Stage 4. They are assessed on their ability to explain their newsletter content and assist peers in understanding it.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially verb emphasis in future contractions, short/long vowels).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (article, event, explain, paraphrase, layout, pictures).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation (summarizing main points, rephrasing ideas).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of visuals to aid understanding.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and engagement in assisting peers.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new thing I learned to explain or share today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did I help my classmates understand our newsletter ideas?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review of the lesson and connection to future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why is it important to explain things clearly to others, especially for our classroom newsletter?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did I do well when I was explaining or clarifying today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What specific strategies helped students explain main points and resolve misunderstandings effectively (e.g., use of visuals, rephrasing prompts)? What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next time we work on a collaborative project or when students need to mediate information? (e.g., more practice with sentence frames for rephrasing, more diverse visuals). Did students meet the learning outcomes related to summarizing with visuals and assisting in resolving misunderstandings?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the unit on "Our Classroom News" by consolidating all skills into the project. The next lesson will transition to a new theme, likely starting with new vocabulary and listening skills, building on the collaborative and communicative competencies developed here.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>